<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="{{@site.locale}}">
<head>

    {{!-- Basic meta. Everything advanced — Open Graph, structured data,
         canonical URLs, the accent colour, members and search — comes from
         {{ghost_head}} at the foot of this block. --}}
    <title>{{meta_title}}</title>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

    {{!-- FIRST, and inline: sets data-theme before the first paint. --}}
    {{> "theme-init"}}

    {{!-- The faces are declared inside the compiled stylesheet, which the
         browser only discovers after fetching and parsing it — one round trip
         later than it could start. Preloading closes that gap, which is what
         keeps the fallback swap from being visible on a cold load.

         Only the two that draw ABOVE THE FOLD: the sans and the mono. The
         icon font is left to be discovered normally, because an icon arriving
         100ms late is invisible where a reflowing paragraph is not.

         `crossorigin` is required even though these are same-origin — fonts
         are always fetched in CORS mode, and a preload without it produces a
         second, unused download rather than a warm cache hit. --}}
    <link rel="preload" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin
          href="{{asset "fonts/switzer-var-latin.woff2"}}">
    <link rel="preload" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin
          href="{{asset "fonts/roboto-mono-var-latin.woff2"}}">

    <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="{{asset "built/screen.css"}}">

    {{ghost_head}}

</head>
<body class="{{body_class}}">

    {{!-- FIRST element inside <body>, per NSDS's skip-link template. The
         target needs id="main" AND tabindex="-1" — without the tabindex,
         Safari and Chrome move the viewport but leave focus behind, and the
         link accomplishes nothing. --}}
    <a class="ns-skip-link ns-visually-hidden" href="#main">{{t "Skip to content"}}</a>

    {{!-- ── CHROME DISPATCH ──────────────────────────────────────────────
      A reading surface must not get a marketing bar. The reading kinds carry
      their own chrome inside their own partial — the training rail, the
      course player — so this only decides whether the SITE bar appears.

      abstract/02 is the reference; abstract/18 owns the NAMES, and it
      supersedes the older list abstract/02 was written against.
    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── --}}
    {{#is "post"}}
      {{#post}}
        {{#has tag="#lesson, #training-lesson, #training-module, #docs-page, #docs-section"}}{{else}}
          {{> "chrome/topnav"}}
        {{/has}}
      {{/post}}
    {{else}}
      {{> "chrome/topnav"}}
    {{/is}}

    {{> "chrome/nav-sheet"}}

    <main id="main" tabindex="-1">{{{body}}}</main>

    {{#is "post"}}
      {{#post}}
        {{#has tag="#lesson, #training-lesson, #training-module, #docs-page, #docs-section"}}{{else}}
          {{> "chrome/footer"}}
        {{/has}}
      {{/post}}
    {{else}}
      {{> "chrome/footer"}}
    {{/is}}

    {{!-- One bundle, built by gulp. All of it is NSDS's own script layer and
         all of it is progressive enhancement — the sheet, the theme controls,
         the curriculum rail, the outline, the reading line and the code copy
         button all degrade to something that works. --}}
    <script src="{{asset "built/main.js"}}" defer></script>

    {{ghost_foot}}

</body>
</html>
